        },
        {
            "timestamp": 1773151300,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "garybeihl",
                "email": "garybeihl@microsoft.com",
                "username": "garybeihl"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 7:\n\nYes - Good catch \u2014 a str_len value larger than 64 causes pldm_msgbuf_insert_array to read past the buffer. The decode side is implicitly protected (it passes PLDM_FIRMWARE_MAX_STRING as the buffer size to pldm_msgbuf_extract_array), but the encode side does not have that guard.nnThat said, this is outside the scope of this patch (which fixes the str_type encoding size). I can submit a separate follow-up patch that adds bounds checks to both encode_get_firmware_parameters_resp and encode_get_firmware_parameters_resp_comp_entry \u2014 would that work?"
        },
        {
            "timestamp": 1773204717,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "Mayank Metha D",
                "email": "mayank.metha@intel.com",
                "username": "mmetha"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 7:\n\n(1 comment)"
        },
        {
            "timestamp": 1773233754,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "garybeihl",
                "email": "garybeihl@microsoft.com",
                "username": "garybeihl"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 7:\n\nPatch Set 7:\n\nMayank: You're right that the spec (DSP0267 v1.3.0) defines version string lengths as uint8 with \"up to 255 bytes.\" The current PLDM_FIRMWARE_MAX_STRING = 64 is an arbitrary limit for static storage, not a spec-defined one. Changing it to UINT8_MAX would be the spec-correct fix, but it increases the size of every pldm_firmware_string struct from 66 to 257 bytes, which affects stack-allocated structures across the firmware update API. That's a bigger change that should be its own patch with Andrew's input on the ABI implications.\n\nThe str_len bounds check in the separate patch (88176) protects the current code against overflow of the 64-byte buffer. If PLDM_FIRMWARE_MAX_STRING is later raised to 255, the bounds check will still be correct (it checks against the constant, not a hardcoded 64)."
        },
        {
            "timestamp": 1773283510,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "Mayank Metha D",
                "email": "mayank.metha@intel.com",
                "username": "mmetha"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 7: Code-Review+1\n\n(1 comment)"
        },
